  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 7: Every single bit of every word is wrong"
            prompt="This chip's memory test fails almost every word it checks - and when a word is wrong, it's not just a bit or two off, the whole byte usually comes back looking unrelated to what was written. There's no obvious per-pin pattern to it (it's not consistently one DQ line). What kind of mistake corrupts an entire byte lane uniformly like that?"
            hint="Check this chip's per-bit DQ deskew settings in its editor (the small indicator under each DQ column, or the parameters themselves) - are all eight bits skewed by about the same amount, or does one stand out from the rest?"
            explanation="All eight DQ bits are skewed by the same amount - a single, shared DQS-to-byte-lane timing error, which is exactly what write levelling exists to correct and clearly never ran (or was undone by a board/trace change since). Because every bit lags identically, the chip captures essentially the *previous* word's data for the whole byte rather than the current one - a uniform, whole-byte corruption, not an isolated-pin problem. This is the real, textbook write-levelling symptom: DQS has to be aligned to CK at each individual DRAM to compensate for fly-by trace-length differences between ranks, and until that alignment is trained, every bit in the lane is wrong together, not just one troublesome pin. Contrast this with DDR Homework 8 (per-bit write-DQ deskew), where only a couple of individual pins are off and the rest of the byte lane is fine - a different real training step with a visibly different symptom."/>
</LABCHAIN>
